### 1.1. Ramification and subgroup lifts

Let $I$ and $P$ be inertia and wild inertia. The residue action identifies $G/I$ with a cyclic group. For a uniformizer $\varpi$ of $L$, the map

$$
I\longrightarrow k_L^\times,\qquad
\nu\longmapsto\overline{\nu(\varpi)/\varpi}
$$

is a homomorphism with kernel $P$. Thus $I/P$ is cyclic of odd order. The higher ramification filtration of $P$ terminates; its successive quotients embed in the additive group of $k_L$ by the leading coefficient of $\nu(\varpi)-\varpi$. Consequently $P$ is a $2$-group. A lift $F\in G$ of arithmetic residue Frobenius acts on $I/P$ by squaring, since its residue action is $x\mapsto x^2$. These valuation descriptions apply in residue characteristic two.

For any finite subgroup $H\subset PGL_3(\mathbb C)$ its inverse image $\widetilde H$ in $SL_3(\mathbb C)$ is finite of order $3|H|$ and has the same invariant subspaces as the full inverse image in $GL_3(\mathbb C)$. If $H$ is a $2$-group, a Sylow $2$-subgroup $Q$ maps isomorphically onto $H$. Moreover $\widetilde H=\mu_3Q$; centrality of $\mu_3$ makes $Q$ normal, hence the unique Sylow $2$-subgroup. This gives a canonical lift on $H$, compatible with every conjugation normalizing $H$.

Suppose first that $P\ne1$. Apply this lift to $\rho_i(P)$, obtaining ordinary representations $A_i:P\to SL_3(\mathbb C)$. The irreducible finite group $\widetilde{\rho_1(G)}$ permutes the $P$-isotypic components transitively: otherwise a proper orbit sum is invariant. All components therefore have the same dimension and multiplicity. Irreducible degrees of a finite $2$-group are powers of two, by the standard degree-divisibility theorem ([Teleman, §13, Theorem 13.6](https://math.berkeley.edu/~teleman/math/RepThry.pdf); see also the [repository source note](../../../Library/FiniteGroups/teleman2005representation.md)). Each common irreducible degree divides three, so is one. There are either one or three isotypic components. In the first case $A_1(P)$ is scalar; a scalar in $SL_3(\mathbb C)$ has order dividing three, so its $2$-power order makes it trivial. Faithfulness excludes this. Hence there are three distinct character lines, transitively permuted by $G$.

At $p\in P$, projective conjugacy of $\rho_1(p)$ and $\rho_2(p)$ gives

$$
A_2(p)=\zeta H A_1(p)H^{-1},\qquad\zeta\in\mu_3.
$$

Both sides without $\zeta$ have order dividing $\operatorname{ord}(p)$, so $\zeta^{\operatorname{ord}(p)}=1$ and $\zeta=1$. Thus the ordinary characters of $A_1,A_2$ agree. Ordinary finite-group character equivalence supplies one conjugator aligning them on all of $P$; this is the character interface of [Fang–Han–Sun, Proposition 2.1](https://msp.org/pjm/2016/283-1/pjm-v283-n1-p05-s.pdf), also used in [the toral source-support proof, §§1 and 2.1](TORAL_CHARACTER_TRIPLE_SOURCE_SUPPORT.md#21-scalar-normalization-and-character-multiplicities). Canonical lifting implies that both images of each $g$ implement the same conjugation $p\mapsto gpg^{-1}$ on these aligned lifts. Distinct characters force the same permutation of the three lines. Both maps are therefore monomial with one common transitive permutation map $\pi:G\to S_3$.

If $P=1$, inertia is cyclic. Its inverse image in $SL_3(\mathbb C)$ is abelian, being generated by a lift of a generator and central scalars. Its weight spaces are permuted transitively by $\widetilde{\rho_1(G)}$. A single weight would make $\rho_1(I)$ trivial. Then faithfulness gives $I=1$, so $G$ is cyclic, contradicting projective irreducibility: a lift of a cyclic generator has an eigenline. There are consequently three distinct weight lines. Inertia fixes them individually, while the cyclic quotient $G/I$ acts transitively through $C_3$. The matrix for $F$ is a monomial three-cycle, whose cube is scalar. Faithfulness gives $F^3=1$ and $G/I=C_3$. The squaring action on $I$ now gives $x^8=x$, so nontrivial $I$ has order seven. Conjugacy at a generator aligns $\rho_2|_I$ with $\rho_1|_I$. The determinant-one lift of each value having order dividing seven is unique: two such lifts differ by $\mu_3$, and their seventh powers remove that ambiguity. Both maps consequently permute the three aligned weight lines in the same way. Again they are monomial with the same transitive $\pi$.

